  "worked_solution": "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 5-\\frac{1}{2}t & =0 \\\\ t & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme values using the second derivative test. The second derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-\\frac{1}{2}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{\u2033}(10)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (5-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(4)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(4)-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{2}+C & =3 \\\\ 20-4+C & =3\\,\u27f9\\,C=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity is given by $v(t)=5t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}-13.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(10)=5(10)-\\frac{1}{4}(10)^{2}-13=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$",
